# Line endings, settled here rather than left to each cloner's local
# core.autocrlf. .git/config is never pushed, so this file is the only
# thing that governs what someone else's checkout looks like.

# LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform. Not
# just `text=auto`, which would check out CRLF on Windows: everything that
# reads these files here is MSYS2 -- bash, make, gcc, python -- and bash in
# particular fails on a CRLF script with "\r: command not found", which
# points at nothing useful. Keeping one line ending everywhere also means
# a clone on Windows and a clone on Linux are byte-for-byte the same,
# which matters to a project whose central claim is that its output is
# bit-identical across the two.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched: binary test inputs, Textalker images, and audio. Some of
# the reference inputs are byte streams that happen to look like text, and
# a stray line-ending conversion would change what Textalker receives.
*.bin binary
*.wav binary
*.dsk binary

# The GPL text ships to Windows users as a plain file with no extension,
# and goes into the add-on zip exactly as it sits here. Left byte-for-byte
# alone so it stays verbatim in every checkout.
nvda-addon/doc/en/copying -text
